-- ABI v1 decision 3, decode side: the string path ALONE, one process --
   5000 strings of P1.2 per set: the FIVE `string` fields of each element.
   `ResultRaw.opaque_id` is a `bytes` field and the policy does not apply
   to it -- including it was C20, and in the ASCII set its 1,000 values are
   arbitrary bytes that the check arm rejected on the first bad byte, so
   the row understated the cost rather than overstating it.
   `check` validates UTF-8 and rejects; `raw` copies the bytes and does not
   look at them -- which is what a C++ non-validating arm IS. A C++
   std::string holds arbitrary bytes, so there is no lossy-substituting
   path to compare against, unlike Rust's from_utf8_lossy.
   Three validators, one process (C20): `scalar` is what every earlier
   figure in this slice was taken against, `table` is what the codec calls
   now, and `protobuf` is the INCUMBENT'S OWN IsStructurallyValidUTF8 --
   the validator it runs on every `string` field it parses, which is the
   comparison R14 asks for.
